00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:16.960
So, today, we announced
BigQuery BI Engine.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.000
And BigQuery Bi Engine
is really helping users

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.680
to have the scale which they
expect in a BI scenario.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.920
So, in this, the key goal
with BigQuery BI Engine

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.310
is to democratize BI so that
your data analyst and business

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:35.300
analyst can do real-time
interactive analytics at scale

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.260
without having to compromise
on data freshness, or speed.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.010
So BigQuery BI Engine
is an in-memory engine,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.860
in-memory analysis engine,
which is built into BigQuery.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.190
So that allows you to do
sub-second query responses

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.680
for some of your business
intelligence queries

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.900
at high concurrency.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.800
So some of the key aspects
of BigQuery BI Engine is that

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.490
sub-second queries--

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.150
it simplifies the
architecture, now

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.720
you don't have to move
data outside of BigQuery

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.000
to any of your data marts
or run complex extracts.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.430
It's built into BigQuery.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.100
And, lastly, it has
some query optimizations

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.530
done for some of
the BI use cases

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:14.960
so that your queries are
really optimized as you

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.950
start scaling your dashboards.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.345
So let's go into each
of them individually.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.170
So sub-seconds queries.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.890
So BI Engine is a column
oriented in-memory analysis

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.570
engine built into BigQuery.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.310
BigQuery is a column
oriented data store,

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.160
and BI Engine is a column
oriented in-memory engine.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.950
It can scale horizontally
so as more users access

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:35.510
your dashboards and reports.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.580
It can replicate data
horizontally to multiple nodes.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.000
So it can support all the
concurrent queries much more

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.068
faster.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.610
And the last one is
an important one.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.280
BigQuery BI Engine
natively integrates

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.510
with BigQuery streaming.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:50.510
So, as data gets streamed
into your infrastructure,

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.300
into your BigQuery table,
it can do aggregation right

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.060
on the streaming buffer,
so your total latency

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.490
from the event coming into
BigQuery versus showing up

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.060
in your dashboard is in
seconds, not minutes or hours.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.670
So that sort of makes the
whole real-time experience

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:07.730
for dashboards much more faster.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.930
Second one is it's built
on BigQuery storage,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.800
and because of which
it eliminates the need

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.610
for managing any BI servers,
or running extracts, or running

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:17.960
a data mart.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.470
It automatically decides what
kind of data sets to be used,

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:25.130
and it automatically loads it
into an in-memory OLAP cube.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.230
So you don't have to really
create any OLAP engine

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:28.880
or manage that.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.010
We sort of create a super
fast OLAP cube on the fly

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.820
and then expose that
to your dashboards.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.120
And our key goal here is
also to be open to partners,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.690
so we'll have an open API,
which is a simple SQL interface,

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:44.000
so that tools like
Looker, and Tableau,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.330
or any of the open source
tools, can connect to BI Engines

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:51.500
through SQL interface and
still get the same experience.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.770
A last one is smart tuning.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.680
So BI Engine has
a query optimizer

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.680
built in which looks at
your BI specific query

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.000
and optimizes that.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.670
For example, it uses the
right sort of encoding,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.350
it understands what
sort of queries

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.065
you're using so it
can do compression,

00:08:06.065 --> 00:08:08.190
or it may not do compression
based on your group by

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.750
or other clauses.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:13.050
And lastly, all of this capacity
management for BI Engine

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.340
is built into BigQuery
UI starting today.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.280
So if you go to
BigQuery UI, you will

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.260
see a new option
called BI Engine where

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.970
you can decide how much capacity
you need for your BI use cases.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.610
So it automatically takes all
those columns, which you create

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:31.200
from Data Studio, loads it into
memory, and any of the data

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.590
queries which come from
Data Studio itself,

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.299
from the BI Engine video,
we don't use BigQuery slots.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.010
So you sort of
make good trade off

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.130
between what kind of
concurrency or query latency

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.320
you need versus how many
slots you want to use.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.030
So you can make this
seamless trade-off

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.480
based on your use case.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.320
And it's as simple as going and
deciding how much capacity you

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.140
need for the project,
and then it automatically

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.980
does that for you.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.620
And all these metrics
about how much

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.935
data is coming from BigQuery
OLAP cubes is all surfaced

00:08:58.935 --> 00:09:02.347
in Stackdriver so you can
see what is a query latency,

00:09:02.347 --> 00:09:03.930
you can decide how
much of it's coming

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.025
from the in-memory
engine, was a slot

00:09:06.025 --> 00:09:07.650
so you can decide
how much capacity you

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:08.942
want to add more versus reduce.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:12.760
So let's look at--

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:14.500
a quick look at
BigQuery architecture.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.167
This is the BigQuery
architecture today,

00:09:16.167 --> 00:09:18.732
which we have shared
with you for all years.

00:09:18.732 --> 00:09:20.940
It's really what makes
BigQuery a petabyte scale data

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.300
warehouse is the separation
of storage and compute

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.395
with a better word network
which makes what BigQuery is.

00:09:27.395 --> 00:09:29.270
But what we are adding
today, with BI Engine,

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:32.370
is a stateful execution layer
on top of the same BigQuery

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.150
storage.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.130
The stateful execution
layer actually

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:38.010
maintains the state
of all your data sets,

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.290
so that when you access
that from a dashboard tool,

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:45.330
like Data Studio or Sheets, it
can do the smart optimization

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.270
in-memory versus going
back to BigQuery storage.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.600
And it can automatically
maintain this adaptive cache

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.790
between the storage layer
and the in-memory layer.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.190
So let's go one level
deeper and see how it works.

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:00.570
So behind BI Engine there
is a semantic model.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.960
So we create a semantic model
comprising of your metadata.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:08.130
It has a calculation API,
which Data Studio uses today,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:12.790
and it maintains all that
semantic model in memory,

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.420
and when the query comes the
intelligent memory manager

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.400
looks at the query, and looks at
what optimizations can be done.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.740
And if it can be done
within the in-memory layer,

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:22.437
it actually does all
those optimizations

00:10:22.437 --> 00:10:25.020
like picking the right encoding,
picking the right compression

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.520
format, and solves
that from the query.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.770
And it cannot-- if
it doesn't do that,

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:31.690
it automatically
fails over to slots.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:34.200
So it automatically decides
what kind of optimization

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.500
can be done, it'll do it.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.190
If not, it'll use slots.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.690
And that's what makes the
initial sort of small queries

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.140
much more faster.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.980
When I'm talking about
millisecond latency

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:44.480
is what you get
from this engine.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:48.830
So how is the pricing for this?

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.870
The pricing is very similar to
how BigQuery's priced today.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:54.670
There's a on-demand tier
and there's a flat tier.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:57.800
So, for on-demand,
it starts at 1 gig,

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.160
and you can add in
increments of 1 gig capacity.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:05.080
And it's about
$0.0416 GB per hour,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:11.240
or it roughly translates to
$30.36 per gig, per month.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.240
And I said earlier, whenever any
query comes from the BI Engine

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.910
capacity, we don't use
slots, so you're not

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:19.730
paying for the per query cost
that you see in BigQuery.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:22.700
So you make those trade-offs
based on your use case.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.320
And if you're a
flat rate customer,

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.990
we pre-bundled the BI Engine
capacity for every tier.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.380
So if you are a
400 slot customer

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.390
we give you 25 gigs of capacity.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:36.150
If you're a 2,000 slot tier, we
give you 100 gigs of capacity.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:37.910
And if you want
anything more, you

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:42.050
can pay based on the per
gig cost, which is on--

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.620
which is on the left side.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:46.070
So, today, in beta,
we are not enabling

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:49.520
billing so you can experience
BI Engine for your use case

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:50.300
up to 10 gigs.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.480
You can try it out, see how
it works for your use case.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.330
But once we start enabling
billing sometime in July,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:00.200
we will increase the capacity
so you can get bigger capacity

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:02.160
sizes as well.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.750
So we've been using BI Engine
for working with customers

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.930
for last few months, and
we've got some great feedback

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:12.570
and it has helped us improve
this whole engine as well.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.100
So Vendasta is a customer
I met at Next last year.

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.560
We're doing a session
on how billing--

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:20.907
how showing the billing
dashboards in Data Studio--

00:12:20.907 --> 00:12:22.740
I noticed they were a
little slower to load.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:26.090
So we worked with them and
got them on to the BI Engine

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:27.110
alpha program.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.200
They worked with us, and
since then it has dramatically

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:32.750
changed the way that
dashboard latency are, right,

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:35.540
from minutes to-- it has come
down to literally seconds.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:37.040
And they're slowly
going to roll out

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.620
this to their internal
users and as well as

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.850
to the end customers.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.060
So we see that success
with Zalando as well.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:48.630
So Zalando is a e-commerce
leader in Europe.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:51.200
So they had this internal
supply chain dashboard

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:55.020
which all of their suppliers
use to look at their use cases,

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:57.800
how much goods are being
sold on their website,

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:59.510
what channels are effective.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:01.550
And they wanted that very
interactive experience

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:03.920
for their business stakeholders
and product managers

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.570
to make those decisions.

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:07.130
By using the BI
Engine, they were

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.160
able to get to that
level of quality latency.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.470
And they were able to roll
it out to a set of users,

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.045
and today they have about 800
users within the organization

00:13:15.045 --> 00:13:17.170
using Data Studio, and
they're going to expand that

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:18.380
to a lot more users.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.990
Again, going back
to democratizing BI

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:24.500
and making it accessible
to a lot more users.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:28.070
Last one is a Fortune
500 media company.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.350
And they sort of create--

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.630
sort of a news data warehouse
for their journalists

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.910
and editors, so
that they can look

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.210
at what channels are
being used, and what

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:42.260
is the more effective way
to market their news pieces.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.170
So they provided a
dashboard on Data Studio

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.630
to their internal users
running on BI Engine,

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:50.870
and they were able to get a
lot more faster experience

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:53.520
for their users.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.430
So, with that, I also
want to introduce

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:59.240
the next piece of democratizing
BI is connected Sheets.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:03.060
So connected Sheet is a way
you use Sheets as an interface

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:05.450
but, it's backed by BigQuery.

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.510
So it maintains a live
connection with BigQuery.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:11.312
So you've got any
knowledge of SQL--

00:14:11.312 --> 00:14:12.770
with minimum
knowledge of SQL-- you

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:15.530
can just drag and drop,
create pivot tables,

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:20.090
do filtering, apply formulas,
with just few clicks.

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:22.550
And because of the
whole G Suite experience

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:24.260
of sharing and
collaboration exposing,

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:26.930
and share securely your
sheets with lot more users

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:29.185
within the
organization, so that we

00:14:29.185 --> 00:14:31.310
don't have to worry about
exporting data to a sheet

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:33.143
and giving it to different
people or emails.

00:14:33.143 --> 00:14:34.220
You can do that securely.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:36.500
But since it's
backed by BigQuery,

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.750
it can do analysis
on a large data set

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.440
which was not possible earlier.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.010
So we're super excited
about connected Sheets.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:47.900
Again, we'll have beta
sometime in end of 2019,

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:50.480
and at that point
connected Sheet will also

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.430
BigQuery BI Engine as
their underlying engine.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:55.580
So you get the same experience
of subsequent query latency

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:58.940
on a big data set with the
interactive experience,

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:02.810
so that you can roll out your
BI use cases to a lot more users

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.790
within the organization.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.770
So with that, let
me introduce Nikunj.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:11.330
Nikunj is the Chief
Product Officer of AirAsia,

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:13.490
and they've been a great
advocate of BigQuery,

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:16.100
and they've been using all
our early versions of product

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.430
and given us great feedback.

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:20.300
So with that, let me hand
it off to him to showcase

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.310
how BI Engine and
connected Sheets

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:23.885
has impacted his organization.

00:15:23.885 --> 00:15:24.927
NIKUNJ SHANTI: OK, great.

00:15:24.927 --> 00:15:25.899
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:31.120
All right.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.110
So a little bit of
background, AirAsia

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:35.900
is a low cost airline
based in Southeast Asia.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:38.480
We're in our
transformation program.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:41.270
We're working on AirAsia
3.0, which is really

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:44.750
about taking the airline
to the next level, which

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.220
is more than beyond flights.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.235
It is really about
selling customers

00:15:48.235 --> 00:15:49.610
other things that
they might need

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:52.730
in their journey such
as hotels, activities,

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:56.000
and other complimentary things
that go beyond the flight.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.630
And to do that, we
have to really become

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:01.430
a digital airline, really
leverage the data that we have.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:02.870
Over the history
of AirAsia, we've

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:05.083
flown over 500
million passengers,

00:16:05.083 --> 00:16:07.250
and we want to start going
on to the next experience

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:10.820
of personalizing their
experiences in booking ticking,

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:12.080
uh, booketing and ticketing.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.460
And then the second thing is
really in the airline world

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.240
you still see a lot
of paper being used.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:18.860
It's about digitizing
that paper so we

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:22.470
can do a lot more data
flows across the board

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.510
and complete the data loop.

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.850
So we'll talk a little bit
about that as we go on.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.700
But the whole goal is to
get AirAsia to AirAsia 3.0,

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.030
which is really around
going beyond flights

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:36.110
and selling other opportunities.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:38.600
And so, just a little bit
of background on AirAsia.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:43.100
We're a very, very large
airline group in Southeast Asia.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:48.735
Number four by numbers in
Asia, 260 aircraft, 360 routes,

00:16:48.735 --> 00:16:50.040
90 unique destinations.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.820
So unique means that we're the
only ones flying that route

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:57.860
which gives us a great view
on where customers want to go.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.470
140 destinations
in 24 countries.

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.160
We fly around 90
million passengers,

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.030
so, as you can imagine, that's
a lot of personal information,

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.849
but also a lot of
information about customers

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:10.200
that don't actually book
and transact with us,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.190
but we have a lot of
detailed information there.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:15.810
And over the lifetime we
have 500 million customers,

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.170
and our staff is quite
intensive as well.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:23.730
We have 24,000 staff
which we call AllStars.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.480
And so, as AirAsia is going
through the next trajectory,

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.230
we're transforming
from an airline company

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:31.320
to a digital company,
and technology and data

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:33.600
is quite important for that.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.720
We're trying to build a brand
that will go beyond ASEAN,

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.010
and we have a five-year
plan for Asia 3.0,

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:40.830
which is really to
go beyond flights.

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.830
And then we want to be
leading the pack on innovation

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.950
and pioneering, so we're really
using data as a key lever

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:48.370
to be able to do that.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:49.995
And we'll talk a
little bit more on how

00:17:49.995 --> 00:17:51.120
that works within AirAsia.

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:53.070
So in terms of data,
we've been collecting

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:54.810
data for a long time.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:57.330
There's so many places that
you can collect information.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:59.940
You can kind of see how
that is listed out here.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:03.390
There's always emails, loyalty
programs, the actual ticketing,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:07.780
booking, profiles of
customers for what they like,

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:09.330
what they don't
like, do they like

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:10.920
to buy a seat--
we sell a La Carte

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.980
seats, meals, bags-- so we
have a lot of preferential data

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:15.478
on what customers like.

00:18:15.478 --> 00:18:18.020
And, based on the routes, we
see a lot of different behaviors

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:19.800
that are happening,
and we want to be

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:23.470
able to use that data to help
personalize what's going on.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.470
We have social media
activity, with the invent

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:27.993
of mobile we have a lot
more on location tracking,

00:18:27.993 --> 00:18:29.910
and how people are using--
as you can imagine,

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:32.610
people that book on mobile have
a different profile than people

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:33.510
that book on desktop.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.310
So we're taking advantage
of all that data.

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:38.370
We spent a lot of time
for the last three years

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:41.640
really normalizing and putting
all that data into BigQuery

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.230
to be able to build
that foundation so

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.670
that we can make really good
use cases come out of it.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.688
And some of those are
really turning out anew,

00:18:47.688 --> 00:18:49.230
and I'll talk about
a couple of them,

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.660
that the team are working
on, that really help

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.910
for our operations as well.

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.630
So there's a couple
of folks working

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:59.700
in a couple of use cases
around meal planning and things

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:01.260
like that.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:02.690
And then all of this really--

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:03.895
our end goal is really--

00:19:03.895 --> 00:19:06.020
to collect information
throughout the whole journey

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:06.860
as much as possible.

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:09.140
We're very guest obsessed,
we want to really drive

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:10.820
our guest experience higher.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.880
And our goal is to have
a really high NPS score,

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:16.280
and we're using all these
different touch points, really

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:17.680
completing the data loop.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.910
As you can imagine,
many, many airlines just

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:21.440
focus on one part
or another part,

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:22.857
we want to really
focus on the end

00:19:22.857 --> 00:19:25.400
to end journey and really
capture all feedback

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.110
and experience that happens.

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:28.820
When people are just
doing discovery,

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:31.907
which is before search, but also
when they come to the website--

00:19:31.907 --> 00:19:33.740
where are they searching
for, how many times

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:35.907
are they searching, how do
we make this a little bit

00:19:35.907 --> 00:19:38.480
better and faster, how do we
market the right information

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:39.050
to customers.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:42.470
So we really need a
really solid foundation

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.900
to be able to collect that
data, and BigQuery is really

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:48.080
provided us that foundation
and, based on that,

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.780
we're able to customize
and do a little bit more

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:53.670
beyond on the search side but
also on the operation side

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.375
that we're working on there.

00:19:56.375 --> 00:19:57.750
And then from the
data management

00:19:57.750 --> 00:19:59.042
to data driven outcomes, right?

00:19:59.042 --> 00:20:01.380
So we have a lot of
data that comes in.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.388
We have descriptive
analytics, which are really

00:20:03.388 --> 00:20:05.430
around business intelligence
and reporting, which

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.280
is standard what
most companies have,

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:09.870
and where we're
making it a lot easier

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.460
and trying to
democratize that data.

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:13.050
So I'll show you
some of the examples

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:17.250
that we have with Data
Studio and in Sheets,

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:18.850
connected Sheets,
how that works.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:21.030
We have some other
situations, on the left,

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:23.430
on diagnostic analytics,
which is really around

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.160
how to improve the operations
or how to sell more.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:28.860
We're working on what might
happen, which is around--

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:32.448
do we put extra meals on a plane
or do we keep the amount there.

00:20:32.448 --> 00:20:33.990
Because every time
we put extra meal,

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:35.610
we think we might
be able to sell more

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:38.320
by just doing more
announcements, and et cetera,

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:39.840
but that's not always the case.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.230
And can we use ML to
be really optimize

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:45.060
our wastage on the plane, to
get some really good examples

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.280
and use cases beyond
that, and then

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:48.640
what's the next best action.

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:52.080
So, depending on your
demographic and your profile,

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.600
if you're flying a
short haul flight that's

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:58.968
only less than 75 minutes, would
you really want to buy a meal,

00:20:58.968 --> 00:21:01.260
or would you be more interested
in boarding and leaving

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:02.040
that plane first.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.728
And so, based on
that information,

00:21:03.728 --> 00:21:05.520
we can make different
techniques and status

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.982
based on your preferences and
also show different results

00:21:07.982 --> 00:21:08.940
to a user on a website.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:12.210
So we've incorporated all
those type of techniques

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:12.940
onto the website.

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:14.440
So if you do use
AirAsia.com, you'll

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:15.820
see many of those things.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.340
And if you're on a plane
that's being leveraging a ML

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:24.030
model for food, you'll
see that the food wastage

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.740
is very low on that
plane because we're

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.250
able to ensure that we don't
have extra weight because

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:30.570
of the food, but extra--

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:32.737
we're not losing money
because of the wastage share.

00:21:32.737 --> 00:21:34.590
So that's all being
taken care of.

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.300
So we're really using
our data and trying

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.850
to drive outcomes
that come out of it.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:42.370
And so the key things
that happen on the AirAsia

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:43.662
transformation is really--

00:21:43.662 --> 00:21:45.870
we want to-- like I mentioned
before, the airline has

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.140
a lot of paper processes.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.530
So we need to go ahead and
digitize that information.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:51.730
Once we start digitizing
the information,

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:53.400
we can start to see
patterns, right?

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:57.030
And we can really start
making minor adjustments to it

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:59.290
and to be able to go
optimize that stuff.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.020
So, as part of that
transformation,

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:03.300
we've gone through
organizational changes

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:04.800
to enable that process.

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:07.560
We've started to move a lot
of our data to the cloud.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.260
Once the data is
in the cloud, we

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:12.210
can do a couple of
automations to really check

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:14.860
on our hypotheses and see
if that's really happening.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:18.480
We move to agile structure
so that commercial teams,

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.730
and marketing teams,
and technology teams,

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.850
are really moving quicker and
being able to produce outcomes

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:25.232
and really--

00:22:25.232 --> 00:22:26.940
instead of taking
months to do something,

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:30.420
we can do something in weeks
to see if our hypotheses are

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:31.910
working correctly.

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:32.910
And then we do it again.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.260
So it's a full circle process.

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:38.680
If we find something else,
we continue to process there.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:40.430
And why do we chose--

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:42.835
or choose Google Cloud Platform?

00:22:42.835 --> 00:22:44.460
Well, back when we
started the journey,

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:48.090
BigQuery was the leader
in the space back then,

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:51.540
and still is the leader now
in terms of processing data

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.370
subsection queries.

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:55.560
Actually, getting information
into BigQuery is very

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.018
straightforward and I'll talk
a little bit about that one-.

00:22:58.018 --> 00:23:01.260
And cost, as a low cost airline,
cost is quite important for us

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:06.100
in being able to decouple query,
and storage is quite important.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.760
And then the NoOps
model of BigQuery

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:13.040
is great so that our developers
can go ahead and develop

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:16.440
infrastructure, or data
sets, but also ETL processes.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:19.260
So they don't have to worry
about an infrastructure

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.840
guy who's spinning up a new
database, that's not a problem.

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:25.440
And then being able to do many,
many queries in background,

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:28.950
so they're not waiting for
whose priority of the query

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:29.880
goes first, right?

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:31.200
That's not a problem anymore.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.030
That was a problem
that we had before.

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:35.790
And so all of that is solved
by the Google Platform.

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.160
But as we develop more
and more things on Google,

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:40.620
all the data sits in one
place, and then we're

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:45.310
able to combine it in BigQuery
to be able to do a lot more use

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:45.810
cases.

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:46.500
All right?

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:50.858
And so our solution architecture
here is built this way,

00:23:50.858 --> 00:23:52.650
and I'll walk you
through a little bit more

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:54.450
so you understand how it works.

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:55.590
We have a RDBMS--

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:58.390
I think most customers
have RDBMSs out there--

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:04.980
our RDBMS is a very old Windows
technology RDBMS, I'll say,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.210
and we need to get
the data out of there.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.290
And so we have some connectors
with the ETL over here,

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:13.380
in the middle we use
Composer, in that scenario,

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:16.150
to get data out of there
and we load into BigQuery.

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:18.828
We have flat files that come
out for many of the third party

00:24:18.828 --> 00:24:21.120
data that we have when we're
doing pricing comparisons.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.670
We use third party
acquisition sources

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.140
and we get some data from there.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:27.390
Again, that goes
through a Pub/Sub

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:30.240
so that we can automatically
load it into BigQuery

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:32.340
via a notification.

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:35.760
We have data from Firestore,
for our NoSQL data,

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:37.800
and that goes through
Cloud Functions whenever

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.380
updates happen, so that
goes into BigQuery,

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:42.120
and any other data
that's automatically

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:45.810
produced from GCP.

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:48.870
So our App Engine logs, and
other things around there,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.260
we can go ahead and
actually put into BigQuery,

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:54.690
and it makes it a lot easier for
us to have a centralized place.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:58.560
And then what we do from there
is really have some processes

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:04.650
on Cloud Functions, or
Composer, or other scripts

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:08.430
that we have that really start
massaging that data into data

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.700
marts that we need, or
big data lake in BigQuery,

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:13.990
so we can start looking
at individual profiles.

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:15.570
So we can look at
the individual user

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:17.183
and see their
history and pattern,

00:25:17.183 --> 00:25:18.600
if you've logged
in, to understand

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.100
what we need to personalize.

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:22.940
Or we go ahead and
do attribute scoring

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:24.660
that we need to
do to provide you

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:27.960
a route, what that route really
wants, or an example of cart

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:31.290
loading is based on who's
flying, what type of meals

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:33.210
might be needed to sell that.

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:34.580
All of that goes into BigQuery.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:38.700
And, for archiving purposes, we
store a snapshot of every day

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.370
into Cloud Storage, so if
we want to roll back in time

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:44.490
and see what happened, we
can go back and go load

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.695
up that snapshot from
Cloud Storage so that--

00:25:46.695 --> 00:25:50.340
a lot of times things,
as you can imagine,

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:53.490
the airline is a moving state
operation and things that

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.590
were the status three
weeks ago might not

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:57.090
fit the status of a ticket.

00:25:57.090 --> 00:25:58.050
And you don't want to--

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.300
you don't want to store a
live repository of that-- we

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:02.990
can just go snapshot in time and
see what that ticket state was

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:05.410
a couple of weeks before and
see what's happening there.

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:08.280
Now, all of that actually goes
into our delivery and digestion

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:09.260
insights delivery.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:14.010
We have two primary tools,
Data Studio and Sheets.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.873
And, recently, we've
been able to cut down

00:26:16.873 --> 00:26:18.540
on some of the other
tools that we use--

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:20.782
just the products have
gotten so much better--

00:26:20.782 --> 00:26:22.240
and they are
continually improving.

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:25.430
And they are helping us reduce
our dependency on third party

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:25.930
tools.

00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:27.840
So we have Data
Studio and Data Sheets

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:29.700
on that, and then
out of BigQuery

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.410
we're able to do
machine learning.

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:35.340
We use some of that data,
again, for personal experiences

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:36.810
with App Engine.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:42.780
It goes into the other GCP core
tools as Dataflow and Compute

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:43.280
Engine.

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:46.502
Dataflow we kind of use
really for how many people

00:26:46.502 --> 00:26:48.210
are searching for a
route, and et cetera,

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:50.390
and we can really do a lot
of stuff with that data

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.490
there that comes in real time.

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.160
That's kind of how
it works there.

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:57.410
And then we'll go into a demo.

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.660
And I'll show you some of
the two products that we use

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:05.140
and how they're
used in real life.

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:07.640
So one of the dashboards that
our marketeers use quite a lot

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.080
is the Nationality
Flown dashboard.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:12.560
I think we saw a demo of that.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.990
The data itself has been
changed a little bit,

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.200
so for anyone that
wants to know,

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:21.650
we've changed all the airports
out so what you're seeing is--

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.170
in some of the
metrics out-- we've

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:26.150
changed some of that out,
so it's not real AirAs--

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:28.730
it's AirAsia data, but
the names are US names,

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:30.110
so it's very easy.

00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:31.610
So, if you know
airport codes you'll

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:33.612
probably see very
familiar names here.

00:27:33.612 --> 00:27:35.570
But this dashboard is
really used by marketeers

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:38.060
to go ahead and know
what type of people

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:41.720
are flying on a flight, what
is happening in terms of number

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:44.120
of passengers flown,
and typically they

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:48.173
were getting this dashboard
via email once a month.

00:27:48.173 --> 00:27:50.090
And now, when we moved
it over to the BigQuery

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:51.780
engine and pipelines
that we have,

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:55.010
we're able to go ahead
and distribute this daily.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:56.352
And customers, and er--

00:27:56.352 --> 00:27:58.560
and clients can go ahead
and drill down and use this.

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:00.830
So I'll go to example here,
we have the date range,

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:04.790
but if I was interested in--

00:28:04.790 --> 00:28:07.130
let's go ahead and
say MY nationality,

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:08.540
I can just click on that.

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:11.390
It's using the BI Engine in
the background and filters

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:12.930
everything pretty quickly.

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:15.650
And if I was interested in
this Fort Lauderdale route,

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:19.250
I can go ahead and click on that
and in subseconds it goes ahead

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:21.410
and change the data across.

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:24.770
Which is fantastic, because,
when you're in a meeting

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:27.812
you can refer to this
dashboard as the Bible

00:28:27.812 --> 00:28:29.520
and everyone's looking
at the same thing.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:32.390
So you can get up to
many, many users on it.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:35.060
This is a copy of the real
one, so obviously there's

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:37.880
only one person on it right
now, but when we're in meetings

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:40.400
you can get up to, like,
a lot of people on it.

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:45.840
We have around a thousand folks
in marketing and commercial,

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:49.760
so you can imagine we can
get about 200 people looking

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.823
at this on a daily basis.

00:28:51.823 --> 00:28:53.990
Not all concurrently, but
concurrently I usually see

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:55.790
around 50 to 60 people using.

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:58.470
And now with BI Engine,
the way that works,

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:00.440
it really makes it easy
and fast for people

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:02.600
to go ask that
question and leave

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:06.530
the meeting with real concrete
action items versus actually

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:08.927
waiting for a report back,
and asking a question,

00:29:08.927 --> 00:29:10.760
and then having another
meeting on a report.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:12.770
So it works quite well.

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:14.860
Let me just reset everything.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:17.450
And you can see here,
as I filter along,

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.810
it's pretty responsive
and fast on how it works.

00:29:20.810 --> 00:29:22.930
So it's quite impressive for us.

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:26.060
But, a lot of times,
the pre-cam dashboards

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:28.465
that we have aren't
enough for our marketeers.

00:29:28.465 --> 00:29:30.090
They want to ask more
questions, right?

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:33.650
So the ultimate tool
that they use still

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:35.003
is a spreadsheet, right?

00:29:35.003 --> 00:29:36.920
Because they want to see
some underlying data,

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:39.590
and they want to do a couple
more things, so what happens

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:44.710
is our BI teams that usually get
tasked with data pukes, right?

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:49.220
And the data puke is really
an extraction for information

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:50.840
because it's really
hard for them

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.510
to go write a SQL query
to get this information.

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:57.080
With the upcoming release
of connected Sheets,

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.720
we can go ahead and provide
a lot more self-service.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:03.330
So this is a demo sheet
that we put together.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:06.563
We'll look at some of the
questions that we have here.

00:30:06.563 --> 00:30:08.480
And with some of the
questions is, it's great,

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:10.520
the dashboard looks great,
but we need something

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:12.800
more on capacity
impact slow, so I'll

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:14.270
show you an example of that.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:16.160
In the dashboard,
it doesn't have that

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:17.720
very easily for someone to see.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:20.990
It's there, but it's not
100% exactly in the form

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:23.500
that they want, and they'll
ask some particular questions.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:26.330
So I'll just go through an
example of what they do.

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:28.745
The data that we're
going to use is--

00:30:28.745 --> 00:30:30.620
we're going to use this
data set here, so let

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.480
me just go to the live one.

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:35.300
And you can see it's
around 12 million rows.

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:37.940
We have a lot of data sets that
are about 100 million rows as

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:38.812
well.

00:30:38.812 --> 00:30:40.520
And then you can
imagine a marketeer will

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:43.850
have a tough time taking
this and putting this

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:44.840
into a spreadsheet.

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:47.400
It's just too heavy for
a spreadsheet to use.

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.780
So how do we solve that
with connected Sheets?

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:55.000
I'll try a brand new one.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.760
So I'll just show
you the process.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:59.810
So if you go to data, data
connectors, connected sheet

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:01.950
here--

00:31:01.950 --> 00:31:05.640
let's see if it is fast enough.

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:08.330
There's a lot of people on Wi-Fi
here, so it's a little slow,

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:10.040
but we'll go to
our demo data set.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.050
And so it's-- as you can tell
it's going straight against

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:20.650
BigQuery here, and
going against that,

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:22.797
and it actually
pulls the data here.

00:31:22.797 --> 00:31:23.380
This is great.

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:24.400
This is the raw data.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:26.530
It doesn't show you
all 12 million rows,

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:28.970
it just gives you an
idea of what's going on.

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:31.380
And then we'll go ahead
and do a pivot table,

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:32.795
as most marketeers want to do.

00:31:32.795 --> 00:31:34.420
And the question that
they want to know

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:38.830
is, Fort Lauderdale,
how has it been growing

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:41.050
in terms of pax nationality,
and we can kind of

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:43.670
add that for the last two years.

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:47.890
So we can go ahead and add
some rows to show that.

00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:53.020
And what we'll do here is group
it by month, add some columns,

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:55.630
and group it by year.

00:31:55.630 --> 00:32:00.730
And we'll add in pax flown,
as that, was the question.

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:10.152
And then we'll add in a
filter that the text contains.

00:32:10.152 --> 00:32:12.970
And so, if you know airport
codes, Fort Lauderdale is FLL,

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:15.250
I think.

00:32:15.250 --> 00:32:18.110
I'm not really in the US
market, but I think that's it.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:20.590
And if you hit the
apply button, it

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:22.390
does some magic
behind the scenes

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:24.610
and you get some
information there.

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:27.460
And you can see how
that actually flows out,

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:31.660
and you can ask more
questions very easily here.

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:35.450
But the magic behind this is,
really it did execute a query--

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:37.900
and if we go to
our query history--

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:41.240
whoops, should I refresh?

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:42.370
Yeah, let me refresh.

00:32:46.555 --> 00:32:47.930
Hopefully it won't
take too long.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:51.415
If it doesn't--

00:32:51.415 --> 00:32:52.540
I shouldn't have refreshed.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:54.960
I should have tapped it.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:58.470
You can see here it did the
query that's here, and then--

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.580
if you-- previously this
type of analysis and work

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:06.600
would have required
a data analyst

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:08.940
to go ahead and write this
query for the marking guy,

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:10.180
and that causes a delay.

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:12.660
And then you have to go ahead
and prioritize some effort

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:13.160
here.

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:14.940
But, now, with
this type of work,

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:19.020
you're able to execute the query
on the fly in a connected Sheet

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:21.330
so that they can get
this type of information

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:22.330
very fast and easy.

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:24.240
And they can continue
on with their questions

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:27.840
without interrupting the
more complex work that a BI

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:30.300
analyst might need to go do.

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:33.827
So this is the next evolution
of data analysis at AirAsia,

00:33:33.827 --> 00:33:35.910
and we'll be able to do a
lot more democratization

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:38.340
of the data that we've
already flattened and built

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:39.540
in BigQuery.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.200
So that's the end of the demo.

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:44.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:50.780
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:33:58.030 --> 00:34:00.400
And then what this really
means in terms of the impact

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:03.580
is we can use the data
in BigQuery to reduce

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:05.800
our operating costs,
like the example I told

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:10.719
you is really wastage on meals,
we can go ahead and solve that.

00:34:10.719 --> 00:34:14.350
Zero hours in terms of managing
servers, we don't have to wait,

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:18.179
we are actually-- everything's
up and running faster.

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:21.610
Previously, in most ETL jobs, if
you're in the data warehousing

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:25.880
world, you only reprocess x
amount of days in history.

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:28.239
With the infrastructure
that we have in place,

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:31.449
I put two years of data,
but we can go many years,

00:34:31.449 --> 00:34:34.090
and we can process
that in minutes.

00:34:34.090 --> 00:34:38.320
Usually we can do the whole
ETL job very, very fast.

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:40.300
Historically, most
companies only

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:42.639
process seven years or 30
days of-- or seven days

00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:44.860
or 30 days of historical
data on a run.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:46.944
We can go ahead and
reprocess the whole cube.

00:34:46.944 --> 00:34:49.210
So that allows us
to keep the raw data

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:51.100
and then go ahead
and run our ETL

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:54.730
pipeline so that we get faster
corrections or faster reports

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:55.540
out.

00:34:55.540 --> 00:34:57.550
And then all of this
can be shared and used

00:34:57.550 --> 00:35:00.040
by the 1,500 plus knowledge
workers that we have.

00:35:00.040 --> 00:35:02.110
We have a lot of
folks that are running

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:03.290
the operations of the plane.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:05.623
They don't need really access
to a lot of this raw data.

00:35:05.623 --> 00:35:09.020
But, as we expose a lot more
data about customer behavior,

00:35:09.020 --> 00:35:10.688
et cetera, we'll be expanding--

00:35:10.688 --> 00:35:12.980
everyone will be able to
access a lot more information.

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:15.250
So, your cabin crew, they're
serving your hot meal,

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:16.880
will know your
preference, whether you

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:18.460
like a Coke or a Diet Coke.

00:35:18.460 --> 00:35:19.980
You don't have to
ask that question.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:22.570
They'll just give you
that beverage of choice

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:25.180
that you need, because that's
where people are expecting

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:26.120
that type of behavior.

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:28.370
If they've been ordering
Coke for the last five years,

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:30.170
you kind of know what they want.

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:33.160
So we can go ahead and
distribute this data a lot more

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:34.160
around the scenes there.

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:35.660
VINAY BALASUBRAMANIAM:
Well, thanks.

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:36.370
Thanks Nikunj.

